  9	
 10	// The sheet's settings: the clerk limit (a clerk settles only claims whose due is at or below it;
 11	// blank: no limit), the floor (a claim's live due is at least it; saved when its field is filled —
 12	// a blank floor field leaves it as it is and saves the clerk limit), the terms (whole business days; a claim's
 13	// due-by is that many business days after its date, skipping weekends and the sheet's holidays, fixed when the claim is created; saved when its field is filled, together with any other field filled),
 14	// each grade's own terms (whole days; a new claim whose person's grade on its date has them uses them instead; saved like the terms; none: the sheet's terms), the late percent
 15	// (two decimals; an overdue claim gains it of its figure for every full 30 days overdue; saved like the terms), the policy date (a claim dated
 16	// before it rounds its live figures half up, one on or after it half to even; blank: the sheet's today; saved when its field is
 17	// filled or was the last one entered, together with any other field filled), the period numbering from (from that date,
 18	// a claim opened is numbered within its period; blank: off; saved like the policy date), the cap (two decimals; a person's
 19	// due-total is at most it; blank: none; saved like the policy date) and the role each user name holds (clerk unless set to steward or viewer — a viewer sees everything and changes nothing).
 20	// The clerk limit, the floor, the terms, each grade's terms, the late percent and the cap are dated: each has a from-date
 21	// field (blank: the sheet's today) and is saved as its value from that date, keeping the values from other dates; each
 22	// shows the value in force on the sheet's today. The proof threshold (two decimals; blank: none; saved like the cap) is dated
 23	// too: a claim whose due is above the one in force on its date needs a proof to be settled or approved. So is the note
 24	// threshold (two decimals; blank: none; saved like the cap): a claim whose due is above the one in force on its date cannot be opened with an empty note. A claim takes the floor, late percent and terms in force on its date.
 25	// The permission matrix (not dated): for each of open, settle, void, reassign, close and reopen, the roles that may take it,
 26	// comma separated (blank: the standing rule — open, settle, void and close by clerk and steward, reassign and reopen by steward);
 27	// saved like the cap. The grace days (not dated; whole days; blank: off; saved like the cap): when the sheet's today is changed,
 28	// every open period whose to-date plus them is before the new today is closed. The entry date (not dated; a date; blank: off;
 29	// saved like the cap): while set, it is the default date of a new claim (the claim form, the quick-add bar, a pasted line
 30	// with a blank date, a correction) in place of the sheet's today.
 31	// A steward delegates the steward role to a user for a from-date and a to-date, inclusive (blank: the sheet's today): while the
 32	// sheet's today is inside them the user acts as a steward in every rule. Anyone but a steward (by the role set), or a to-date
 33	// before the from-date, is refused.
 34	// Each grade's grace days (dated; whole days; blank: none; saved like the cap): a claim whose person's grade in force on its
 35	// date has grace days in force on that date is overdue only once its due-by plus them is before the sheet's today, and its
 36	// late charge's 30-day boundaries count from its due-by plus them. Its due-by is shown as it is.
 37	export function SettingsPanel() {
